course: dictionary
lesson: 1251
title: Burglar and its word family
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A burglar is a person who breaks into a house, shop or building to steal things. You can work out this meaning from the words around it, such as break in and steal.
explain: «Burglars check under doormats, so the hiding place of a spare key is kept secret.» The sentence tells you that burglars look in hiding places, so a key hidden under a mat is not safe. Try burglar in the sentence in place of the hard word and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Some words are made by chopping an ending off a longer word. This is called back-formation. «Burgle» was made from «burglar» by removing the ending, and burgle means to break in and steal.
fact: A burglar is a person who breaks into a house, shop or building to steal things.
fact: Burglar is a noun, and burglars is the plural, meaning more than one burglar.
fact: Locking doors and windows helps to stop a burglar getting in.
fact: A burglar alarm protects a home by making a loud noise when someone breaks in.
fact: «Burgle» was made from «burglar» by chopping off the ending, and it is a back-formation.
exercise: What is a burglar? | A cartoon animal that washes its food | *A person who breaks into a house, shop or building to steal things | A person who guards a museum and keeps it tidy | A kind neighbour who helps carry the shopping
exercise: «Burglars check under doormats, so the hiding place of a spare key is kept secret.» Why is a spare key kept secret? | So it can be sold in a shop | So the postman can use it every day | *So a burglar cannot find it | So the key stays warm in the sun
exercise: Which word was made from burglar by chopping off an ending? | *burgle | cartoon | burglars | burglar alarm
test: «A burglar alarm rang loudly in the night, and the family woke up.» What does the alarm most likely warn about? | The weather is getting cold | The shop is open late | *Someone may be breaking in | A birthday party next door
test: What does burglar mean? | A person who fixes locks on doors | A person who sells houses to families | A person who cleans windows for money | *A person who breaks into a building to steal things
exam: Why do people lock their doors and windows? | To make the house look bigger from the street | To let the cat in and out whenever it likes | *To keep out burglars who want to steal things | To keep the rain off the carpet
